Don't archive a signature object the backend never emitted.

`ghcjs.tests.backpack.build` fails for ghc914-sh (and only for it) when
the instantiated `consumer` sublibrary is archived:

    Building library 'consumer' instantiated with Module = ...-impl:Mod
    [1 of 2] Compiling Module[sig] ( .../autogen/Module.hsig, .../Module.o )
    [2 of 2] Compiling Consumer    ( consumer/Consumer.hs, .../Consumer.o )
    Linking StaticWay library...
    llvm-ar: error: .../Module.o: No such file or directory

Nothing is misplaced: GHC's `-odir` is the very directory `ar` searches.
GHC announced `Module.o` and wrote no such file -- the JS backend emits
no code for a filled-in signature, whose implementation lives in the
other unit.  `getHaskellObjects` builds the archive list from
`allLibModules`, and `explicitLibModules` includes `signatures:`, so the
object is demanded whether or not the backend produced one.

Ask the file system rather than assume, and only for signature modules:
a missing object for an ordinary module is a real failure and must keep
reaching `ar` as one.

Scope, which is what points at this rather than at the JS backend: the
same test passes for ghc914-sh on native and wasi32 (where GHC does emit
the object), and passes on ghcjs for every mainline compiler -- ghc9124,
ghc9141, ghc967, ghc984, on both platforms.  Those mainline jobs run the
v1 builder, and their logs show why they never hit it: for an
instantiated component they compile the two modules and go straight to
installPhase, with no archive step at all.

--- pa/src/Distribution/Simple/GHC/Internal.hs	2026-08-27 19:31:23
+++ pb/src/Distribution/Simple/GHC/Internal.hs	2026-08-27 19:31:23
@@ -90,7 +90,7 @@
